Alright, I’ve just created the core files for the UI, battle system, and overworld. The overworld module now contains a fairly large chunk of code handling tile rendering, player movement, NPCs, etc. I need to review the newly added overworld.js to ensure the rendering logic is correct and consistent with the rest of the engine.

Let me recall what the earlier version of the overworld drawing loop looked like. In the original code (from before I overwrote it), the player sprite was drawn using a few steps:

```js
const sheet = Gfx.SHEETS.hero;
const frames = sheet[P.dir];
const f = P.moving ? frames[1 + (P.stepFrame % (frames.length - 1) === 0 ? 0 : frames.length - 2)] ?? frames[1] : frames[0];
const fi = P.moving ? frames[(P.stepFrame % 2) + 1 >= frames.length ? 1 : (P.stepFrame % 2) + 1] : frames[0];
ctx.drawImage(fi, e.x - camX, e.y - camY - 2);
```

This is a bit tangled: it first grabs the whole sheet object, then extracts the direction array, then computes two different frame selections (`f` and `fi`). It ends up drawing `fi`, but `f` is computed and never used. Also, the logic for selecting the walking animation frames seems off – it mixes `stepFrame` modulo calculations with a conditional that tries to avoid index 0 but ends up with weird offsets. Moreover, the `step` variable (which tracks sub‑pixel movement within a tile) is not considered at all, so the animation timing may be out of sync with the actual walking speed.

I want to simplify this. The rest of the code already tracks `P.step` (the pixel offset within a tile) and `P.stepFrame` (a counter incremented each frame while moving). The sprite sheet for the hero is already structured as `Gfx.SHEETS.hero[direction]` where each direction array contains the frames: frame 0 is the standing pose, frames 1…n are the walking cycle. A common pattern is: if the player is moving and has taken less than half a tile (`P.step < TILE/2`), show the first walking frame; otherwise show the second walking frame; otherwise show the standing frame.

I also notice that the earlier code accessed `Gfx.SHEETS.hero` twice and used a temporary `sheet` variable that is unnecessary. I can replace that with a direct lookup `Gfx.SHEETS.hero[P.dir]`. Then I can compute the frame to draw (`fi`) in a single expression that respects both `P.moving` and the sub‑tile step. Something like:

```js
const frames = Gfx.SHEETS.hero[P.dir];
const fi = P.moving && P.step < TILE / 2
    ? frames[1 + (P.stepFrame % (frames.length - 1))]
    : frames[0];
```

Here, `frames.length - 1` gives the number of walking frames (excluding the standing frame). The modulo cycles through those walking frames as `stepFrame` increments. If the player isn’t moving or has progressed beyond half a tile, we fall back to the standing frame (`frames[0]`). This should produce a smoother, more predictable animation and also eliminates the unused variable `f`.

